A||||16||William||New paint job trashed?||||1||
Z||000000||William||11-11-2000||12:02 PM||||I think I have trashed my 6 month old paint job this week! It was windy this week and the fiero got covered with pine needles. This morning I went out to rinse off the car and noticed that I now have black outlines everywhere that a pine needle had layed. I'll take care of that nonsense....I grabbed a bucket of soapy water and scrubbed the car. Oh dear God, it's not coming off! This can't be good! Next I grabbed some lighter fluid, and scrubbed. I've never seen anything on a car that this stuff won't remove but it did NOTHING! Next I grabbed some paint thinner and still it did nothing! I can't think of anything else except sandpaper :-o..... I know we have some professional painters on the list, and I'm seeking advice. Did this crap eat through the clear coat, or permantly stain it? I've seen this thing happen to abandoned cars that have set for years but this happened in just 2 days. I've lived around pine trees my whole life but I've never had anything like this happen.||||reg||1
Z||000001||deceler8||11-11-2000||12:31 PM||||I wouldn't try anything other than mineral spirits to remove this. Anything harsher might in fact damage the clear coat.<P>Other than that, you might try some swirl/scratch remover. Most of these have enough petroleum distillates in them to remove tar, so they might work.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||11-11-2000||12:35 PM||||You may want to try Meguiars Deep Crystal System step 1 Paint Cleaner.  It removes bonded contaminants & oxidation from the surface.  Since the surface has been thoroughly cleaned, this would be my next step.  Follow the directions on the bottle.||||reg||3
Z||000003||2xsess||11-11-2000||01:12 PM||||I agree with all the above. But, if all that doesn't work, you could use 1000 grit wet sandpaper to get it off then go over it again with 1500 grit. You WILL have to compound it back up to get the shine back so <B>don't</B> do it unless you know someone with a buffer.<P>Good Luck.   <P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||DrRodey||11-11-2000||01:20 PM||||Goo-Gone Is my friend.. I have never had a problem getting pine sap off with it....<P>------------------<BR>'88 Formula CONVERTABLE<BR>'85 Coupe<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://freepages.ugo.com/RodeysPlace/files/fieroPage.JPG">   <BR> <A HREF="http://freepages.ugo.com/RodeysPlace/HomePlace.html" TARGET=_blank>freepages.ugo.com/RodeysPlace/HomePlace.html</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||fropuff||11-11-2000||01:36 PM||||All these are good ideas.  I think I would try some products before sanding it, however.  I just found out how good GS27 scratch remover worked.  It took really deep scratches out of my 3 month old paint.  They were scratches from tree braches, from my little adventure into the woods.  There was also some sap, and it took it right off.  You can still see them just a little, but when I wet sand and buff it again, they will be gone completely.  Hope you get that crap off.  <P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000006||William||11-11-2000||02:23 PM||||Thanks for the tips. If it does come down to sanding and buffing, the whole car would probally just need to be repainted as the black outlines completely cover the hood, roof, trunk and wing. What is really bothering me is how this has happened? Anyone ever have pine needles do this in just a few days? I'm gonna try some of these tips but the bad news is that it really looks like the "fluid" that came from the needles has eaten into the clear coat. Thats some great stuff that Macco uses I suppose!<p>[This message has been edited by William (edited 11-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tigger||11-11-2000||02:36 PM||||I'd think the meguires with a wheel would take it off.  It might take some time to work it off, don't wheel it too hard too quick or you'll burn throught the clear.  Sanding would be my last resort.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Fleabitdog||11-11-2000||04:25 PM||||William. How expensive was your paintjob if you don't mind me asking. I am in Hollywood Fl and am lookling to get my GT painted in a couple weeks. Thanks Dog||||reg||1
Z||000009||deceler8||11-11-2000||05:52 PM||||If it has been repainted recently and you paid a national shop to do the job, there may be a warranty on the paint/application.<P>All the car mfr's have been known to repaint cars due to paint spotting from fallout, etc, so this might be a course of action to you if the paint was defective and/or poorly applied.||||reg||1
Z||000010||strdream||11-11-2000||06:03 PM||||It sounds like you are sure that the sap is gone and what you are seeing is a stain. It's hard to believe that a tree can suck up water, soak in sunshine and use it to make that crap. If it has indeed penitrated the clear and linked with it you are stuck with having to remove the effected clear by some of the means posted above. Just hope that the stain is shallow. Before I got the big guns out and go after it I would like to see what bleech does to it.<P>let us know the end result anyway<P>Ross ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Terrybogin||11-11-2000||06:09 PM||||Use the Meguire's #1 and then #3 polish/compound on a wool buffing pad. It will take the marks off. If not, then try the wet sanding mentioned above. It sounds like you have a single stage paint- if so, you can compound the fool out of it without hurting it too much, but BC/CC will not take too much abuse before cutting through.||||reg||1
Z||000012||William||11-12-2000||10:27 AM||||Tried the goo gone but it really didn't work. Actually it did kinda remove one mark but I had to scrub on one spot for 10 minutes and the area is now hazed. I'm now confident that what I'm seeing is stained clear coat and not sap. Will the swirl / scratch remover handle this or will sandpaper be needed? This is a cheap bc/cc paint job and I think it only got 1 coat of clear....I don't know anyone with a buffer so my options seem limited<p>[This message has been edited by William (edited 11-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||2xsess||11-12-2000||11:36 AM||||William,I'd take it back to Maaco and see what they say before you do anything more. <P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||zeusrekning||11-12-2000||06:54 PM||||I just bought a white coupe and it has that black "stuff" i guess it could be considered oxidization. I was going to use rubbing compund. Will this work to get it off?||||reg||1
Z||000015||William||11-12-2000||07:42 PM||||Problem solved!!!!!! Thank you all for the comments. After my last post I moved up to the bigger guns. First I tried heavy duty rubbing compount. Big mistake! It instantly removed the black marks as well as the clear coat. I headed back to the parts store,(for the tenth time this weekend), This time I went for the Meguires swirl remover and it worked perfectly,I would have never thought it would have been able to pull the stains out of the cleat coat. It also removed the milky blotches next to the black areas. I was even able to use it to polish out the rubbing compound incident. Lessons learned? First, remove all pine  trees in the front yard. lesson 2 is the fact that wax does more than make a car shiney and must be on the car at all times. The last lesson is that being lazy with the car cover is very bad indeed! Guy's, If this kinda thing ever happens to you, save yourself a LOT of money and get the swirl remover first!||||reg||1
Z||000016||Oreif||11-12-2000||08:40 PM||||Now that it is cleaned, I would use Meguires Deep Crystal Polish then a very good coat of Meguires Carnuba wax. A friend has a white 85 coupe that had Meguires on it and some jerk thought it was funny to spray a line with black spray paint on the side. The Meguires cleaner took it right off!||||reg||1
